{
 "thread": "fCMxccTdITY",
 "subject": "Change locale for shinyapps",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2015-03-03T04:45:42Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "N2OE55Qk_R8J",
   "from": "Chris Mulligan",
   "author": "b7954cd5c804c370",
   "date": "2015-03-02T17:31:18Z",
   "subject": "Change locale for shinyapps",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I&#39;ve got a script which grabs a few datapoints from an API, does some data massaging, and then graphs it with ggplot. It works perfectly locally, but it fails on shinyapps because the locale is forced to be &quot;C&quot; and not &quot;en_US.&quot; I&#39;ve tried to set the locale to something else, but apparently (see snippet below) my only options are &quot;C&quot; or &quot;POSIX.&quot;<div><br></div><div><div>    locales &lt;- system(&quot;locale -a&quot;, intern = TRUE)</div><div>    library(stringr)</div><div>    print(unique(str_split_fixed(locales, &quot;\\\\.&quot;, 2)[, 1]))</div><div><br></div><div>It might be avoidable in some circumstances, except that the API I&#39;m working with returns dates like so: &quot;Mon, 02 Mar 2015 05:15:00&quot;. The Month is only listed in the form of the abbreviation, %b in en_US. </div></div><div><br></div><div>Any suggestions on workarounds?</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "9byvaBugBzAJ",
   "from": "Paul de Barros",
   "author": "a8c73113f8e105ae",
   "date": "2015-03-03T04:30:22Z",
   "subject": "Re: Change locale for shinyapps",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">The problem seems to go beyond just the locale.  It appears that the server running <a href=\"http://shinyapp.io\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://shinyapp.io&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792363852585000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1hfLMoFI9mwomNFr-rhxav\">shinyapp.io</a> does not recognize month names (at least as input), and so it probably does not recognize month abbreviations, either (though I have not checked that).  <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/28656683/r-script-working-locally-not-working-on-shinyapp-io\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://stackoverflow.com/questions/28656683/r-script-working-locally-not-working-on-shinyapp-io&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792363852585000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3Sas3y80GT1diZ9RUlaw7a\">See here</a> for more details.<div><br></div><div>My suggestion would be to change the string to reformat it as a numerical date, and reassemble it.  <a href=\"https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-patched/library/base/html/strptime.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-patched/library/base/html/strptime.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792363852585000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3izB5XojROIVECiUTHV_gT\">This page</a> gives an enormous list of date formats.  Yours appears to be &quot;%a, %d %b %Y %X&quot;, but you want it to be &quot;%d %m %Y %X&quot;.  I would be to use str_replace from the <a href=\"http://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/stringr/stringr.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/stringr/stringr.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792363852585000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3uSixsD6So6cX5fVojPTys\">stringr package</a>.  Replace everything like &quot;Mon, &quot; with nothing, and replace everything like &quot;Mar&quot; with the relevant month number.  This will probably require 19 separate str_replace statements (one for each month and each day of the week).  Also, str_replace only works on one string at a time, but you can probably get around that using <a href=\"https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/base/html/Vectorize.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/base/html/Vectorize.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792363852585000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3dM9ImFI4Ig6A2FMEeLMZz\">Vectorize</a>.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div>On Monday, March 2, 2015 at 12:31:18 PM UTC-5, Chris Mulligan wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I&#39;ve got a script which grabs a few datapoints from an API, does some data massaging, and then graphs it with ggplot. It works perfectly locally, but it fails on shinyapps because the locale is forced to be &quot;C&quot; and not &quot;en_US.&quot; I&#39;ve tried to set the locale to something else, but apparently (see snippet below) my only options are &quot;C&quot; or &quot;POSIX.&quot;<div><br></div><div><div>    locales &lt;- system(&quot;locale -a&quot;, intern = TRUE)</div><div>    library(stringr)</div><div>    print(unique(str_split_fixed(locales, &quot;\\\\.&quot;, 2)[, 1]))</div><div><br></div><div>It might be avoidable in some circumstances, except that the API I&#39;m working with returns dates like so: &quot;Mon, 02 Mar 2015 05:15:00&quot;. The Month is only listed in the form of the abbreviation, %b in en_US. </div></div><div><br></div><div>Any suggestions on workarounds?</div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "z6ajaVZZhlcJ",
   "from": "Chris Mulligan",
   "author": "b7954cd5c804c370",
   "date": "2015-03-03T04:45:42Z",
   "subject": "Re: [shinyapps-users] Change locale for shinyapps",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">Thanks for the suggestion. That&#39;s about what I ended up doing this afternoon, although using gsub. Which meant only 13 gsubs, and no need to Vectorize myself. <div><br></div><div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;Jan&quot;, &quot;1&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;Feb&quot;, &quot;2&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;Mar&quot;, &quot;3&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;Apr&quot;, &quot;4&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;May&quot;, &quot;5&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;Jun&quot;, &quot;6&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;Jul&quot;, &quot;7&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;Aug&quot;, &quot;8&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;Sep&quot;, &quot;9&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;Oct&quot;, &quot;10&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;Nov&quot;, &quot;11&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;Dec&quot;, &quot;12&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$dump_time &lt;- gsub(&quot;(Mon|Tue|Wed|Thu|Fri|Sat|Sun), &quot;, &quot;&quot;, df$dump_time)</div><div>    df$time &lt;- as.POSIXct(df$dump_time, format=&quot;%d %m %Y %H:%M&quot;)</div><div><br><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
